@anon.3c70fe6116b9432998eba4bc16a060ab.99 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [5047 x i8] }> <{ [5047 x i8] c"### Operands\0A\0A- bs=BYTES : read and write up to BYTES bytes at a time (default: 512);\0A   overwrites ibs and obs.\0A- cbs=BYTES : the 'conversion block size' in bytes. Applies to the\0A   conv=block, and conv=unblock operations.\0A- conv=CONVS : a comma-separated list of conversion options or (for legacy\0A   reasons) file flags.\0A- count=N : stop reading input after N ibs-sized read operations rather\0A   than proceeding until EOF. See iflag=count_bytes if stopping after N bytes\0A   is preferred\0A- ibs=N : the size of buffer used for reads (default: 512)\0A- if=FILE : the file used for input. When not specified, stdin is used instead\0A- iflag=FLAGS : a comma-separated list of input flags which specify how the\0A   input source is treated. FLAGS may be any of the input-flags or general-flags\0A   specified below.\0A- skip=N (or iseek=N) : skip N ibs-sized records into input before beginning\0A   copy/convert operations. See iflag=seek_bytes if seeking N bytes is preferred.\0A- obs=N : the size of buffer used for writes (default: 512)\0A- of=FILE : the file used for output. When not specified, stdout is used\0A   instead\0A- oflag=FLAGS : comma separated list of output flags which specify how the\0A   output source is treated. FLAGS may be any of the output flags or general\0A   flags specified below\0A- seek=N (or oseek=N) : seeks N obs-sized records into output before\0A   beginning copy/convert operations. See oflag=seek_bytes if seeking N bytes is\0A   preferred\0A- status=LEVEL : controls whether volume and performance stats are written to\0A   stderr.\0A\0A  When unspecified, dd will print stats upon completion. An example is below.\0A\0A  plain\0A    6+0 records in\0A    16+0 records out\0A    8192 bytes (8.2 kB, 8.0 KiB) copied, 0.00057009 s,\0A    14.4 MB/s\0A  \0A\0A  The first two lines are the 'volume' stats and the final line is the\0A  'performance' stats.\0A  The volume stats indicate the number of complete and partial ibs-sized reads,\0A  or obs-sized writes that took place during the copy. The format of the volume\0A  stats is <complete>+<partial>. If records have been truncated (see\0A  conv=block), the volume stats will contain the number of truncated records.\0A\0A  Possible LEVEL values are:\0A  - progress : Print periodic performance stats as the copy proceeds.\0A  - noxfer : Print final volume stats, but not performance stats.\0A  - none : Do not print any stats.\0A\0A  Printing performance stats is also triggered by the INFO signal (where supported),\0A  or the USR1 signal. Setting the POSIXLY_CORRECT environment variable to any value\0A  (including an empty value) will cause the USR1 signal to be ignored.\0A\0A### Conversion Options\0A\0A- ascii : convert from EBCDIC to ASCII. This is the inverse of the ebcdic\0A  option. Implies conv=unblock.\0A- ebcdic : convert from ASCII to EBCDIC. This is the inverse of the ascii\0A  option. Implies conv=block.\0A- ibm : convert from ASCII to EBCDIC, applying the conventions for [, ]\0A  and ~ specified in POSIX. Implies conv=block.\0A\0A- ucase : convert from lower-case to upper-case.\0A- lcase : converts from upper-case to lower-case.\0A\0A- block : for each newline less than the size indicated by cbs=BYTES, remove\0A  the newline and pad with spaces up to cbs. Lines longer than cbs are truncated.\0A- unblock : for each block of input of the size indicated by cbs=BYTES, remove\0A  right-trailing spaces and replace with a newline character.\0A\0A- sparse : attempts to seek the output when an obs-sized block consists of\0A  only zeros.\0A- swab : swaps each adjacent pair of bytes. If an odd number of bytes is\0A  present, the final byte is omitted.\0A- sync : pad each ibs-sided block with zeros. If block or unblock is\0A  specified, pad with spaces instead.\0A- excl : the output file must be created. Fail if the output file is already\0A  present.\0A- nocreat : the output file will not be created. Fail if the output file in\0A  not already present.\0A- notrunc : the output file will not be truncated. If this option is not\0A  present, output will be truncated when opened.\0A- noerror : all read errors will be ignored. If this option is not present,\0A  dd will only ignore Error::Interrupted.\0A- fdatasync : data will be written before finishing.\0A- fsync : data and metadata will be written before finishing.\0A\0A### Input flags\0A\0A- count_bytes : a value to count=N will be interpreted as bytes.\0A- skip_bytes : a value to skip=N will be interpreted as bytes.\0A- fullblock : wait for ibs bytes from each read. zero-length reads are still\0A  considered EOF.\0A\0A### Output flags\0A\0A- append : open file in append mode. Consider setting conv=notrunc as well.\0A- seek_bytes : a value to seek=N will be interpreted as bytes.\0A\0A### General Flags\0A\0A- direct : use direct I/O for data.\0A- directory : fail unless the given input (if used as an iflag) or\0A  output (if used as an oflag) is a directory.\0A- dsync : use synchronized I/O for data.\0A- sync : use synchronized I/O for data and metadata.\0A- nonblock : use non-blocking I/O.\0A- noatime : do not update access time.\0A- nocache : request that OS drop cache.\0A- noctty : do not assign a controlling tty.\0A- nofollow : do not follow system links." }>, align 1
